    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. one concern i have is the potential for \"dark corners\" in the collection—basically, items that don't fit easily into a specific category and end up being misfiled or just getting lost in the system because the classification is too rigid. how do these two systems compare when it comes to handling those kinds of disorganized or outlier items?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Both systems can present challenges with outlier items, but LCC is often more amenable to these \"dark corners\" through its extensive use of *addendums* and nuanced Cutter numbers. DDC, being more prescriptive with its numbered blocks, can sometimes force diverse items into a somewhat restrictive structure. For truly unique or cross-disciplinary outliers, using LCC, perhaps in combination with robust Dublin Core metadata, allows you to assign classification while still capturing the item's unique descriptive data separately. This hybrid approach mitigates the rigidity issue in both systems.",
      "source": "target",
