    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "so with the LCC and the hybrid approach, is there a specific way to categorize things that are completely unidentifiable? i'm worried about having pockets of the archive that are just... chaotic or untamable, where the organization just breaks down entirely and makes the whole section feel like a mess.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When facing completely unidentifiable or chaotic material, standard classification becomes impractical, and this is where established archival practices supersede strict cataloging. You should group these items under a designated \"Unidentified Materials\" or \"Unclassified Holdings\" heading within the LCC framework, creating a distinct physical and virtual locus for them. Crucially, supplement the classification mark with detailed *physical description* metadata, noting provenance gaps or unknown content, rather than forcing a subject classification. This transparency allows future researchers and curators to assess the chaotic material systematically without pretending it fits the rigid system.",
      "source": "target",
